+# Contributing
+
+Thanks for contributing to `awesome-best-ai-tools-for-recruiters-2026`.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+- Every submission must be a public GitHub repository.
+- Every submission must include a direct `https://github.com/owner/repo` URL.
+- No proprietary SaaS products.
+- No closed-source demos, landing pages, or product sites without a public repo.
+- The project must be relevant to recruiters, hiring workflows, sourcing, resume parsing, screening, matching, or self-hosted ATS workflows.
+- The project must have a visible open source license.
+
+## Submission Format
+
+Please include:
+
+- Repository name
+- Direct GitHub URL
+- 2-4 sentence summary based on the repository README
+- Star count
+- Primary language
+- License
+- Last commit date
+- Best use case for recruiters
+
+## Quality Bar
+
+- Prefer recruiter-native projects first.
+- If the category is thin, adjacent open source building blocks are acceptable, but say so explicitly.
+- Do not submit abandoned repos unless they are still historically important and clearly labeled.
+- Do not inflate weak categories with unrelated AI tools.
+
+## Pull Requests
+
+Before opening a PR:
+
+1. Confirm the repo is public and still accessible.
+2. Confirm the license is visible on GitHub.
+3. Confirm the tool is relevant to recruiter workflows.
+4. Keep the README format consistent.
+
+## Issues
+
+Open an issue if:
+
+- A listed repo has become private, archived, or unavailable.
+- Metadata such as stars, language, or license is outdated.
+- A better open source GitHub repository should replace an existing entry.
